session:pr657Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #633 asked to wire the newly-landed owl_functional_parser (.ofn -> owl_ontology::Ontology, from #180/#627) into the CLI and notebook kernel, split out the same way Manchester's .omn CLI wiring (#161) was split from its own parser (#139). Rather than designing a new integration shape, the prior Manchester wiring (PR #292) was read first and mirrored line for line: dagalog::load_file gained a .ofn branch alongside .omn that materialises ABox-only via owl2rl2datalog::assert_abox (load_file's contract is 'add quads,' not 'reason'); apply_ontologies/compile_ontology_rules special-case .ofn the same way, additionally compiling the TBox via owl2datalog and batching those rules with every other ontology source, because (mirroring #177's still-open Manchester gap) a Functional-Style TBox axiom has no RDF triple representation and would be lost if not compiled at parse time. The one internal refactor was renaming parse_manchester_file to parse_frame_ontology_file(ext, path) with a match on 'omn'/'ofn', instead of duplicating the file-read-and-dispatch boilerplate a third time. dagalog-kernel got a new %%functional <path> cell magic (cell/functional.rs, execute_functional_file) that is a near-verbatim copy of cell/manchester.rs's execute_manchester_file, including reusing PR #374's RdfTranslationReport-based skipped-ABox-assertion warning in the returned status string unchanged. Test fixtures (a small SubClassOf+ClassAssertion .ofn snippet, and an ObjectUnionOf non-atomic ClassAssertion for the skip-reporting test) were written directly from owl_functional_parser's own integration-test syntax examples rather than guessing the grammar. All new tests passed on the first run once the code compiled, since the implementation is a structural mirror of already-working, already-tested Manchester code rather than new logic." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:657 ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr657 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .
